# Python Port Plan — Step 10 · A Standard Tool Library

## Purpose

Port `week1_baseline/ruby/10_standard_tool_library` to Python, producing
`week1_baseline/python/10_standard_tool_library` that is behaviourally
equivalent: `Boukensha.run`/`Boukensha.repl` gain a standard library of
tools registered automatically instead of by hand — `Tools::FileSystem`
(sandboxed file I/O: `pwd`, `list_directory`, `read_file`, `write_file`,
`delete_file`, `search_files`), `Tools::Shell` (`run_command`, with a
timeout and an optional executable allow-list), and `Tools::Mud` (a full
CircleMUD gameplay tool set built on the separate `mud_manager` gem).

This is the eleventh and final entry in the `docs/plans/python_port/<step>`
series for `week1_baseline` (see [`context.md`](context.md) for the
index). `09_global_executable` is the direct predecessor (not yet ported
at the time of this writing — see [`09_global_executable`](
09_global_executable)); this step restores the two Ruby-side regressions
that step introduced (see below — genuinely good news this time) and adds
three new tool modules plus their `Context`/`Boukensha.run`/`repl` wiring.

**This step has one prerequisite outside the numbered `week1_baseline`
series that the other ten plans in this index do not: `mud_manager` itself
needs its own Python port before `Tools::Mud` can be implemented for
real.** See the dedicated section below — this is flagged prominently
because it changes the shape of "done" for this step alone.

Confirmed via `diff` against `09_global_executable`: `agent.rb`,
`prompt_builder.rb`, `run_dsl.rb`, `logger.rb`, `errors.rb`, `registry.rb`,
`tool.rb`, `message.rb`, `client.rb`, `config.rb`, `tasks/*.rb`,
`backends/*.rb` are **byte-identical**. `context.rb`, `repl.rb`,
`version.rb`, `boukensha.rb`, `boukensha_loader.rb`, and `boukensha.gemspec`
each gain real, meaningful changes. Three new tool files and a new
`examples/example.rb` are added (this step regains an `examples/`
directory, absent since `08_the_repl_loop` — see `09_global_executable`'s
plan for why it had none).

## The `mud_manager` dependency (read this before estimating scope)

`Boukensha::Tools::Mud` (`lib/boukensha/tools/mud.rb`) is a thin
tool-registration layer over a **separate gem**,
`week0_explore/mud_manager` (`MudManager::Session` — a long-lived telnet
connection with background buffering and IAC stripping, ~268 lines;
`MudManager::Primitives` — a stateless library of ~50 typed CircleMUD
command builders, ~418 lines). `boukensha.gemspec` declares `spec.
add_dependency "mud_manager", "~> 0.1"` in this step — a real, resolvable
dependency (unlike `09_global_executable`'s more provisional-looking bare
`gemspec` line in its `Gemfile`), confirmed by the fact that this repo's
own `.boukensha` setup actually runs a live MUD session against it
(verified independently while debugging `bin/ruby/10_standard_tool_
library` earlier in this repo's history).

`mud_manager` has **no Python port and no plan for one anywhere in this
`docs/plans/python_port/` series** — `00_config`'s plan originally flagged
it as "not yet folded into a numbered `week1_baseline` step... gets its
own plan once it lands there"; it has now landed, in this exact step.
**Recommend treating the Python port of `mud_manager` as its own,
separate, standalone package** (mirroring Ruby's structure exactly: a
sibling package outside the `week1_baseline/python/<NN>_*` numbered
series, e.g. `week0_explore/mud_manager_py/` or similar, exposing a
`Session` class and a `primitives` module with the same ~50 command
builders) **written and planned independently before or alongside this
step's `Tools::Mud`**, rather than embedding a from-scratch telnet client
inside this plan. This plan covers `Tools::Mud`'s registration/wiring
layer assuming a Python `mud_manager` package already exists with an
equivalent public surface; it does **not** design that package's port.

