## Behavioural rules carried over unchanged

1. **Every `FileSystem`/`Shell` path argument is resolved relative to the
   sandboxed root and checked for escape** — `resolve(path)` expands the
   agent-supplied path against `root`, then requires the result to equal
   `root` or start with `f"{root}/"`; anything else returns the **string**
   `"error: path '...' escapes the working directory"` rather than raising
   — the agent sees the error as a normal tool result and can retry, same
   error-as-string convention already established for tool-call failures
   in `06_the_logger`'s plan. This is not Python exception-based error
   handling by design; port the string-return convention exactly, not a
   raised `ValueError`.
2. **Every `FileSystem` tool's real file-I/O exceptions are caught and
   turned into the same `"error: ..."` string convention** (`rescue => e;
   oops.call(e.message)`), not left to propagate up to `Agent`'s
   already-existing tool-dispatch error handling (`06_the_logger`) — this
   is a second, tool-internal layer of error-to-string conversion, in
   addition to (not instead of) the Agent-level `rescue StandardError`
   safety net.
3. **`search_files`'s regex compilation failure is reported as a tool
   result, not a crash** — an invalid pattern string returns `"error:
   invalid pattern: ..."` rather than propagating Ruby's `RegexpError`
   (Python: catch `re.error` from `re.compile(pattern)` the same way).
4. **`Shell#run_command`'s allow-list check is a first-token-only string
   match**, not a shell-aware parse — `command.to_s.strip.split(/\s+/).
   first` against `allowed_commands` (stringified) — a deliberately naive
   guard, not a security boundary meant to withstand adversarial input
   (e.g. it does nothing to stop `"git ; rm -rf /"` if `git` is allowed).
   Port the same naive check faithfully; do not "improve" it into a real
   shell-parsing guard, since that would be inventing security posture the
   Ruby source doesn't claim to have.
5. **`run_command` always returns combined stdout+stderr**, trimmed, with
   an `"\n[exit N]"` suffix appended only on non-zero exit — a genuinely
   empty (but successful) output becomes the literal string
   `"(no output)"`. Command-not-found and timeout are both reported as
   `"error: ..."` strings, not raised exceptions (matching the same
   convention as `FileSystem`).
6. **`Tools::Mud.register` auto-connects at registration time**, not on
   first tool call — a failed auto-connect is caught and only `warn`'d to
   stderr (does not raise, does not prevent registration from completing);
   every gameplay tool's `guard` lambda then checks `session.open?`,
   returning `"error: not connected — call mud_connect first"` if the
   auto-connect failed or a later disconnect occurred, rather than raising.
7. **`Tools::Mud`'s tool blocks catch `ArgumentError` from `Primitives`
   command builders** (raised for invalid enum-like arguments — direction,
   position, mode, etc. — per `mud_manager`'s own `check_enum!`/
   `require_str!` validation) and convert to an `"error: ..."` string, same
   convention as everywhere else in this step. Not every tool has this
   guard (`flee`, `practice`, `save_character`, `send_raw` don't wrap in
   `begin/rescue ArgumentError`, since their underlying `Primitives`
   methods take no validated enum arguments) — port the presence/absence
   of this rescue per-tool exactly as Ruby has it, not uniformly added to
   every tool.
8. **`Boukensha.run`/`.repl`'s tool auto-registration is keyed on
   truthiness of `working_dir` and on `mud != false`,** not on `mud`
   being non-`None` alone: `mud: nil` (the default) means "check config
   for `mud_host`+`mud_username`, register if both present"; `mud: false`
   explicitly means "never register MUD tools regardless of config";
   an explicit `mud: {...}` hash always registers, bypassing config
   entirely. `working_dir: false` means "skip `FileSystem`+`Shell`
   entirely"; any other truthy value (including the default `Dir.pwd`)
   registers both.
9. **`mud_opts_from_config` requires *both* `cfg.mud_host` and `cfg.
   mud_username`** to be present before returning a non-`nil` options
   hash — `mud_host` alone (which always has a default of `"localhost"`
   per `Config#mud_host`'s `|| "localhost"`) is not sufficient; recall
   `Config#mud_host` **never actually returns `nil`** (it falls back to
   `"localhost"` unconditionally), so in practice this guard only ever
   turns on whether `cfg.mud_username` (no default, `nil` if unset) is
   present — port the two-part `&&` check faithfully even though one side
   is effectively always-truthy, rather than simplifying it to a
   single `mud_username`-only check that would be functionally identical
   but not textually faithful to the Ruby source.

## Design decisions to make explicitly

- **Ruby's per-registration closures (`resolve = lambda do |path| ... end`,
  shared across all six `FileSystem` tools) become a small private
  helper — either a nested closure inside `register()` (direct
  translation) or a tiny non-exported class instantiated once per
  `register()` call.** Recommend the direct nested-function translation
  (`def _resolve(path): ...` defined inside `register()`, capturing
  `root` via closure) over introducing a class, since Ruby doesn't
  introduce one either and a class would be an unrequested structural
  addition — same "don't invent an abstraction Ruby doesn't have" principle
  applied throughout this port.
- **`Tools::Mud`'s single shared `MudManager::Session` instance, captured
  by every tool's closure** — the direct Python translation is the same
  nested-function-with-closure pattern as `FileSystem`/`Shell`, capturing
  a single `session = mud_manager.Session(host=host, port=port)` created
  once inside `register()`. No need for a class here either, matching
  Ruby's own choice not to wrap this in an object.
- **The `mud_manager` Python package's expected public surface** (to be
  designed in its own separate plan, referenced but not designed here):
  a `Session` class with `open()`/`open?`→`is_open`/`close()`/
  `send_command(cmd)`/`drain()`/`read_until_prompt(timeout=None)`/
  `read_until_quiet(quiet_seconds=1.0, timeout=None)`/`login(username,
  password)`, and a `primitives` module (or class of `@staticmethod`s,
  mirroring Ruby's module-level functions taking no `self`) with the ~50
  command-builder functions `Tools::Mud` calls through `p.<name>(...)`.
  Recommend the Python port of `mud_manager` mirror this shape closely
  (module-level functions, not a class needing instantiation, matching
  Ruby's `MudManager::Primitives` being called as `p = MudManager::
  Primitives; p.look(...)` — a module used as a plain namespace) — flagged
  here only so `Tools::Mud`'s own port has a concrete target shape to code
  against once `mud_manager`'s real plan exists; the actual design
  decisions for `Session`'s telnet/threading/buffering internals belong in
  that separate document, not this one.
- **`working_dir: Dir.pwd` as a Ruby default-argument-value is evaluated
  once per call** (matches every other per-call keyword default in this
  port, e.g. `06_the_logger`'s `Logger.new` note) — Python:
  `working_dir: str | Path | None | Literal[False] = None` in the
  signature, with `if working_dir is None: working_dir = os.getcwd()`
  resolved inside the function body, **not** `working_dir: str =
  os.getcwd()` in the signature itself (which would freeze the cwd at
  Python *import* time — module load — rather than at each call, a much
  worse divergence than the usual mutable-default trap since it would
  silently use whatever directory the process happened to `import
  boukensha` from, not whatever directory the caller is actually in when
  they call `run()`/`repl()`).
- **The three-way `working_dir` parameter type (`str`/`Path`, `None`
  meaning "default to cwd", `False` meaning "disable entirely") has no
  single clean Python type** — recommend a `Path | Literal[False] | None`
  annotation (or a plain runtime check without strict typing, if the
  project isn't running a strict type checker across this port) rather
  than inventing a sentinel object; `False` specifically (not `None`) is
  the Ruby source's own chosen "disabled" value (`working_dir: false`),
  so Python should keep `False` as the disable sentinel too, for call-site
  fidelity with the Ruby examples/README (`working_dir: false` in Ruby
  reads naturally as `working_dir=False` in Python).

## Testing

Ruby's `10_standard_tool_library` has no automated tests, only
`examples/example.rb` (a real, live-MUD-server-driven run — genuinely the
hardest example in this whole series to smoke-test without a running
CircleMUD instance). Recommend:

1. Port `examples/example.py` 1:1 for manual smoke-testing — **requires an
   actual reachable CircleMUD server** (the real `.boukensha/settings.yaml`
   already has a working `mud:` block in this repo, per the earlier
   debugging history) to exercise for real; document this requirement
   clearly in the Python README rather than pretending it's a pure-offline
   demo like every earlier step's example.
2. Carry all `09_global_executable` test files forward; extend
   `test_context.py` (`working_dir` field, expansion behaviour, `None`
   default) and `test_repl.py` (banner validation restored — same
   assertions `08_the_repl_loop`'s plan specified — plus the new mud
   status line, with `_probe_mud` mocked/monkeypatched so tests never
   open a real socket).
3. Add `tests/test_tools/test_file_system.py` against a `tmp_path` fixture
   directory, covering all six tools: `pwd` returns the root; `list_
   directory` (default `.`, sorted, directories suffixed `/`, empty →
   `"(empty)"`); `read_file`/`write_file` (incl. creating missing parent
   dirs)/`delete_file` round-trip; `search_files` (literal + regex
   patterns, `glob:` filtering, `path:line:content` format, invalid regex
   → error string, no matches → `"no matches"`); and, for every tool, a
   `../`-escaping path argument → the specific escape error string, not an
   exception.
4. Add `tests/test_tools/test_shell.py` with real subprocess execution in
   a `tmp_path` (no mocking needed — these are genuinely fast, offline
   commands): a plain command's combined stdout+stderr; a non-zero exit →
   `"[exit N]"` suffix; a command producing no output → `"(no output)"`;
   an `allowed_commands` allow-list rejecting a disallowed first token
   without executing it at all; a command that exceeds `timeout` → the
   timeout error string; a nonexistent executable → the "command not
   found" error string.
5. Add `tests/test_tools/test_mud.py` against a **fake/mocked
   `mud_manager.Session`** (a test double implementing the same interface,
   no real socket) covering: successful auto-connect at `register()` time;
   auto-connect failure is swallowed (warning only, registration still
   completes, tools are still registered); every gameplay tool when
   disconnected returns the "not connected" guard string instead of
   calling through to `send_cmd`; an `ArgumentError`-raising `Primitives`
   call (e.g. an invalid `direction`) surfaces as an `"error: ..."` string
   for tools that wrap it, and is **not** caught for the tools that don't
   (`flee`/`practice`/`save_character`/`send_raw`) — matching the
   per-tool exactness called out in behavioural rule 7.
6. Extend `tests/test_run.py` (from `07_the_run_dsl`'s plan) with:
   `working_dir` truthy → `FileSystem`+`Shell` tools appear in the
   registry; `working_dir=False` → neither does; `mud=None` + config
   without `mud_username` → no MUD tools registered; `mud=None` + config
   with both `mud_host`/`mud_username` → MUD tools registered using
   config values; `mud=False` → never registered regardless of config;
   an explicit `mud={...}` dict → registered using those exact values,
   bypassing config entirely.
7. Extend `tests/test_boukensha_loader.py` (from `09_global_executable`'s
   plan) with: `MUD_NAME` set (with `MUD_PASSWORD`) → `repl()` called with
   the legacy env-derived `mud=` dict and `working_dir=False`; `MUD_NAME`
   set without `MUD_PASSWORD` → aborts with the specific message; neither
   set → `repl()` called with no explicit `mud`/`working_dir` override.

